WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.460
SIERRA: So I’ve always been curious.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.590
What happens when you don’t finish a whole
pack of antibiotics because sometimes you

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.500
feel better halfway through, and they tell
you to take 12, and you’re like, 12?

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.549
That’s way too much.

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:16.800
I’m all set with six.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.800
TALKINGTON: Right.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:23.749
The challenge is if you take a more limited
number then there’s the potential that you

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:30.899
kill off some of the bad bacteria that’s
being targeted, but some of it survives.

00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:34.940
And the bacteria that survives, and the bacteria
that survives then has the opportunity to

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:38.739
learn how to defeat that antibiotic that has
been prescribed.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:43.370
SIERRA: So don’t save the other six and
give it to my friend without health insurance.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:44.780
TALKINGTON: No, please don’t.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.199
Please don’t do that.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:47.199
SIERRA: Noted.

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:49.780
Never will!

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.860
Antibiotics are one of the most important
discoveries in history, and they can seem

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.140
like a magical cure-all.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:01.239
We take them for granted, and we expect them
to fix everything from infected cuts to strep

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:02.239
throat.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:06.630
But bacteria are learning to outsmart antibiotics
on a massive scale.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:12.790
Hundreds of thousands are dying every year,
and that number could soon rise into the millions.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:16.610
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:22.270
Today, what happens when bacteria in our body
become resistant to antibiotics.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.030
And a curable infection suddenly becomes … incurable.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:32.600
CBS THIS MORNING: This morning, new details
on how our food supply is giving rise to antibiotic

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.600
resistance superbugs.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.479
AL JAZEERA: An invisible but deadly threat
has creeped into U.S. hospitals.

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:42.360
BBC NEWS: It will spread quickly … and the
chances that it will spread globally are very

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.360
high..

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.380
SIERRA: You may have heard about this before
on TV or in the news.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.369
Or this may be your first time.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:53.659
Either way, antibiotic resistance can be super
confusing.

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:55.189
I know it was for me.

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:59.259
So before we dig into it, let’s get the
basics straight.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:04.729
Antibiotic resistance doesn’t mean your
body becomes resistant to antibiotics.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:06.140
Bacteria do.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.660
When you’re sick and you take antibiotics,
most of the bacteria get wiped out.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.800
Bam, gone, no more sick days, back to work.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.410
But some bacteria can be really crafty.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.500
They fight off the antibiotic and survive.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.130
There aren’t enough left to make you feel
sick, but once they reproduce, they can turn

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:28.970
into a resistant strain that endangers other
people.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.640
This has a chance of happening any time antibiotics
are used.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.350
The more we misuse them, the stronger the
bacteria become.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:40.890
But in order to understand what a big deal
this is, we probably need to take a trip back

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.870
in time.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.540
So what was life like before antibiotics?

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:53.890
TALKINGTON: The reality before antibiotics
were available was that people could die from

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.170
a simple cut.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.730
SIERRA: This is Kathy Talkington.

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:02.160
She directs the project on antibiotic resistance
at the Pew Charitable Trusts.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.760
Her work is the gold standard in the field.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.840
She spoke to us from Pew’s studio in DC.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.620
TALKINGTON: About a third of the deaths pre-antibiotics
were due to infections, in the United States.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.680
And, you know, when you think about things
like childbirth, a lot of folks were dying

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.820
from that without the advancement of antibiotics.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.300
So things that we very much take for granted
today back then were just a whole different

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.960
reality when they were scared of some basic
infections.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.850
SIERRA: Life was a lot more risky.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:34.840
TALKINGTON: It was.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.160
SIERRA: Bacteria.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.850
Microscopic organisms.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.190
We can’t see them with our naked eye but
they’re literally everywhere.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:46.440
They were the first lifeform on earth, and
they have helped us breathe, eat, and grow

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.310
into the species we are today.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:53.560
If bacteria disappeared overnight, then every
lifeform on the planet would vanish in a matter

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.560
of years.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:00.000
But there’s a flip side; some bacteria are
harmful, and they lead to conditions ranging

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.080
from an infected cut on your finger to diseases
like tuberculosis and the plague; diseases

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.870
that have claimed millions upon millions of
lives throughout history.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.600
TALKINGTON: Antibiotics have often been referred
to as a miracle drug.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:22.030
And the way they work is they kill off the
bacteria by either just killing it or reducing

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.780
its ability to reproduce.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:34.050
Back in 1928, Alexander Fleming is given credit
for finding penicillin, and he was working

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:39.030
in his lab, looking at bacteria, and sort
of by happenstance, when he came back after

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.780
being out for a few days, saw that there was
mold growing in the petri dish, and what he

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:50.610
noticed is the bacteria next to that mold
had died off and what’s interesting is when

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:57.569
he discovered penicillin, and he fully understood
how antibiotics worked, he warned everybody

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:02.719
that we have to use these cautiously, that
resistance will develop.

00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:08.979
FLEMMING: And I have a fear that when penicillin
can be bought over the counter patients will

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:15.259
indulge in over medication, and in many cases
they will not take large enough doses, if

00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:24.030
the dose is too small the microbes will not
cured and they will be educated to resist

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.030
penicillin.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.560
TALKINGTON: And unfortunately, we didn’t
heed that advice as we should have, we had

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:36.830
sort of a heyday or the golden age of antibiotic
discovery where we were staying ahead of the

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:40.000
resistance of antibiotics of bacteria.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.319
Unfortunately, that didn’t last...Because
we have not come up with new what they often

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:49.120
call classes of antibiotics, the bacteria
are outsmarting us.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.270
They’ve defeated or have figured out, in
many cases, how to defeat the drugs that we

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.270
have available.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:00.240
SIERRA: In fact, the world hasn’t come up
with a new “class” of antibiotics since

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.620
the early 1980s.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:07.580
Think of it this way, the last time we struck
a major blow against bacteria, shoulder pads

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:14.590
were in, Russia was still the Soviet Union,
and Blondie was on top of the charts.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:23.389
For more than 40 years, bacteria have been
gaining ground in our race against resistance.

00:06:23.389 --> 00:06:31.449
SIERRA: Why can’t we just keep making new
and better antibiotics?

00:06:31.449 --> 00:06:37.500
TALKINGTON: We currently do not have, enough
pharmaceutical companies investing in research

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:43.259
and development around antibiotics, because
the return is not there.

00:06:43.259 --> 00:06:48.660
When you think about a company trying to decide,
all right, am I going to make a new antibiotic?

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.120
I know it’s going to cost a lot of money
to figure this out and get it through the

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:55.319
pipeline and get it to the patient’s bedside.

00:06:55.319 --> 00:06:59.599
And I know that once I get it across there,
I’m not going to be able to sell it very

00:06:59.599 --> 00:07:02.120
often because we don’t want to use it.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.479
And then you compare that to something like
a chronic disease drug, where the minute it’s

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:12.949
available and it’s better than the most
recent one, the sales are going to be higher,

00:07:12.949 --> 00:07:18.729
the price potentially is higher, and it’ll
be used for maybe a lifetime of that patient.

00:07:18.729 --> 00:07:22.520
So the calculation is very different compared
to other opportunities.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.789
SIERRA: Right.

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:24.789
Money talks.

00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:25.789
Demand talks.

00:07:25.789 --> 00:07:26.789
TALKINGTON: Right.

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:30.939
SIERRA: Drugs that patients will take every
day for the rest of their lives can earn a

00:07:30.939 --> 00:07:32.220
lot of money.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:39.879
Take the arthritis drug, Humira, which registered
19.9 billion dollars in global sales in 2018.

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:42.249
Antibiotics just can’t compete with those
numbers.

00:07:42.249 --> 00:07:45.560
And so there’s not a strong financial reason
to develop them.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:48.930
But let’s say things change in the United
States or Europe.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:54.339
Maybe governments offer big financial incentives
that encourage drugmakers to really dive into

00:07:54.339 --> 00:07:55.439
antibiotioc research.

00:07:55.439 --> 00:08:00.339
There’s every chance that there might be
a breakthrough leading to new, specialized

00:08:00.339 --> 00:08:01.339
antibiotics.

00:08:01.339 --> 00:08:03.559
But that alone wouldn’t solve the problem.

00:08:03.559 --> 00:08:08.279
And that’s because bacteria are developing
new and different resistances all over the

00:08:08.279 --> 00:08:12.219
world, and bacteria like to travel.

00:08:12.219 --> 00:08:17.889
For example, take the resistant enzyme NDM-1,
which is present in several types of bacteria.

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:23.370
ITN News: We’ve all heard of E.coli, but
scientists have found a new bug that can live

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:29.309
inside this and it’s resistant to almost
all antibiotics.

00:08:29.309 --> 00:08:34.760
A new gene NDM-1 has reached the U.K. and
scientists warn it could spread rapidly, as

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:37.310
nothing is being developed to treat it.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:41.660
SIERRA: NDM-1 was first observed in Sweden
in the mid-2000s.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:46.310
But within just a few years, it had cropped
up in over 80 countries.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:50.820
This is just one example of how quickly resistant
bacteria can spread.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.470
TALKINGTON: This is very much an international
issue.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:58.720
This is the issue is that resistance knows
no borders.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:04.280
SIERRA: So we can’t just solve the problem
at home wherever home may be and be safe.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:05.360
TALKINGTON: Exactly.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:11.460
I should say, however, it’s important for
every country to do what they can.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:17.150
We shouldn’t wait for, say, a global solution
to this problem.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:22.510
While that would be the ideal, in the meantime,
everything that each country is doing to address

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:23.600
this will help.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:27.170
But we do need to think about this and look
at this at a global level.

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:31.910
SIERRA: So how is it different, let’s say,
here in the United States versus somewhere

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:34.340
like China or India?

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.710
TALKINGTON: There are different challenges
that each is facing.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:44.630
For example, in China and India, some of the
issues around antibiotics are they may have

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:50.140
them available over the counter for human
use, which we don’t have in the United States,

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:55.850
so how do they begin to address that and making
sure that those antibiotics are getting to

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:56.940
the appropriate people.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:03.010
But they’re having to weigh that reality
with the fact that many people are dying from

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:09.960
infections that could be treated with antibiotics,
so there’s an access problem for some of

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:14.530
these drugs versus the potential excess use
of antibiotics.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:19.340
SIERRA: When thinking about a problem this
big, it’s easy to lose sight of the people

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.270
who have to deal with it every day.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:24.810
The patients who are suffering, and the doctors
who are struggling to treat them.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:29.330
And if you lose sight of this, you kind of
lose sight of the human toll.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:31.830
It seemed like it was time to call a doctor.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:34.320
SATLIN: My name's Michael Satlin.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:39.640
I'm a physician at Weill Cornell medicine
and I see patients at New York Presbyterian

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:45.290
Hospital in New York City and my research
focuses on better understanding the epidemiology

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:50.570
of resistant bacteria and trying to come up
with strategies that might help deal with

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:54.520
how we prescribe antibiotics and manage patients.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:58.570
When somebody presents with a urinary tract
infection we should be able to just treat

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:01.930
that quickly with pills that you can pick
up at your pharmacy.

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:07.040
But because of resistance a lot of times there
are no pills that are available to treat that

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.040
infection.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:09.390
So they have to come to the emergency room.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:12.940
They have to have a procedure where they have
a venous catheter put into their arm.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:15.762
They have to arrange for antibiotics to be
infused at home or sometimes they can't do

00:11:15.762 --> 00:11:16.762
that.

00:11:16.762 --> 00:11:20.250
They have to go to a rehab to have that done
or stay hospitalized.

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.020
There are complications from having it.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:28.010
So I think for every one case where there
is some tragic outcome because of resistance

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:31.660
there are probably 10 cases like that.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:38.470
TALKINGTON: So bacteria are evolving just
as humans evolved.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:44.240
They have ways of adapting that defeat the
drugs that we’ve created.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:50.490
When we use antibiotics in any setting whether
it’s in human setting, medical care, or

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:56.630
in farming it contributes to the development
of antibiotic resistance.

00:11:56.630 --> 00:12:01.820
So in the United States, for example, we’re
using about 70 percent of the antibiotics

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:03.670
in animal agriculture.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:08.740
SIERRA: So we haven’t even mentioned the
other massive part of resistance.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.360
Even if every single person on earth took
antibiotics correctly, and that’s a big

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.490
if, we’d still have a huge problem.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:20.950
And that’s because we are not the only ones
who consume these medications.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:27.230
Frontline: And by using antibiotics in food/animal
production we’re creating drug resistant

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:30.330
pathogens that can then go on to cause drug
resistant infections.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.850
When you put the animals together and have
them confined its alot easier to pass diseases

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:39.790
back in forth, giving them a little bit of
antibiotics seemed to fix that problem, sort

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:41.370
of put a bandaid over it.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:48.220
I am looking at trying to prevent any health
issues in this small city of chickens of 100

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.220
thousand.

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:53.410
SIERRA: Giving antibiotics to animals is common
worldwide, but the question of the right way

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.380
to do it is controversial.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:13:00.590
TALKINGTON: One of the things that we need
to do in animal agriculture is we need to

00:13:00.590 --> 00:13:07.310
implement stewardship principles, and what
I mean by that is we need to have programs

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:13.750
on the farm that drive appropriate use of
antibiotics, to make sure that we’re using

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.240
the antibiotics only when necessary.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:22.690
SIERRA: So if I’m a farmer and I’m using
antibiotics on my herd, am I using it because

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:27.810
my cow is sick, or am I giving it to all of
my cows so that none of them get sick?

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:29.730
TALKINGTON: So it depends.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:33.490
One area that they’ve been used and are
no longer allowed in the United States but

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:38.390
sometimes used in other parts of the world,
and that’s for growth promotion, it helps

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:40.350
to make the animal grow faster.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:42.190
SIERRA: So it has nothing to do with sickness.

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:43.810
It’s just getting a plump chicken?

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:44.810
TALKINGTON: Exactly.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.230
Exactly, in a shorter amount of time.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:48.510
So that’s one category.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:52.690
The second category is basically preventing
the disease.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:58.740
Where you might have a situation where you
know in these circumstances often disease

00:13:58.740 --> 00:13:59.740
crops up.

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:03.550
So you’re going to give an antibiotic in
order to prevent that from happening.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.660
And sometimes it’s called prevention and
control.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:11.730
A control example would be when you might
have one animal that’s sick in the herd,

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.240
and you treat the rest of them to prevent
the spread.

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:21.070
And then the ultimate area is for treatment
when you have a sick animal with a clear indication

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:25.120
of bacterial infection, then antibiotics can
be used for that.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.180
SIERRA: So, why does this matter?

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.910
Well, because when animals develop resistant
bacteria, it can spread to us.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:31.910
And there are a lot of ways.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:36.370
It can reach us through the meat we eat or
when we come into contact with animals.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:39.930
And if you are close enough to an infected
animal, it can even reach us through the air

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:41.650
we breathe.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:46.470
Resistant bacteria can even get into the water
farmers use to irrigate their crops.

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:50.150
TALKINGTON: The resistant bacteria gets into
that water.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:57.120
The water may be then used to irrigate your
crops of lettuce, and someone could then eat

00:14:57.120 --> 00:15:00.040
the lettuce that has the resistant bacteria.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:06.260
And there are many other more complex ways
that that resistant bacteria can be shared

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:11.510
in many different facets before it gets to
a human, maybe in a hospital, et cetera.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.950
The pathway can be complicated, but the science
has been clear that there is a relationship.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:20.290
SIERRA: So even being a vegetarian is not
going to help me defeat this?

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:21.820
TALKINGTON: Exactly right.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:26.170
SIERRA: I have to stop drinking water, stop
eating lettuce, stop petting pigs.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:27.580
I mean, what can I do?

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:28.580
TALKINGTON: Right.

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:35.010
Or we can stop using antibiotics unnecessarily
and reducing the incidence.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.790
SIERRA: So what are we doing wrong?

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:46.780
TALKINGTON: So the unnecessary use of antibiotics
is accelerating the development of antibiotic

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:48.160
resistance.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.860
For example, the World Health Organization
estimates that about 50 percent of the antibiotics

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.360
being prescribed are unnecessary.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.360
SIERRA: Oh, wow.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:16:01.240
TALKINGTON: And, again, the more we use antibiotics,
no matter what setting whether it’s in the

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:06.480
human setting or in animal setting we’re
driving resistance, we’re expediting the

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:08.780
development of resistance.

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:15.990
SATLIN: I had a patient, a young woman with
lymphoma who was undergoing a stem cell transplant.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:21.510
The hope was with this transplant her lymphoma
would be cured and she could lead a long life

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:26.430
and everything was going OK until one morning
while her immune system was weak from chemotherapy

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:29.970
and we were waiting for her immune system
to recover, she told her nurse around 9:00

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:35.730
in the morning that she was short of breath
and by that afternoon her organs essentially

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.180
stopped working.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:41.570
She had a fever, she had to be put on a breathing
machine, she needed medicines to keep her

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:46.500
blood pressure up, at our first sign of fever
we did get blood cultures and we started her

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:50.860
on what we thought were appropriate antibiotics
for the types of infections that we typically

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:51.970
see.

00:16:51.970 --> 00:16:58.070
But unfortunately by the next day she died
and a day after that we actually figured out

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:02.180
what she had, what she was infected with and
she was infected with a bacteria that was

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:07.289
resistant to all the antibiotics that we had
given her.

00:17:07.289 --> 00:17:12.559
TALKINGTON: The most recent statistics we
have from the Centers for Disease Control

00:17:12.559 --> 00:17:19.259
is about 23,000 people a year in the United
States are dying from resistant infections.

00:17:19.259 --> 00:17:24.939
And I think the number globally is about 700,000
according to the World Health Association.

00:17:24.939 --> 00:17:27.559
TALKINGTON: And, those are older numbers.

00:17:27.559 --> 00:17:31.299
The Centers for Disease Control is expected
to update that.

00:17:31.299 --> 00:17:34.990
So I think, unfortunately, we’ll see those
numbers going up.

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:40.559
SIERRA: Since I spoke with Kathy, the Centers
for Disease Control did in fact release new

00:17:40.559 --> 00:17:41.559
figures.

00:17:41.559 --> 00:17:46.899
They found that 2.8 million resistant infections
occur in the United States each year, and

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:50.470
that the number of annual deaths had risen
to 35,000.

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:55.539
SATLIN: There have been some estimates that
have been made, that if we continue our current

00:17:55.539 --> 00:18:01.730
track by 2050 there'll be 10 million people
a year who die from antibiotic-resistant bacteria.

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:02.730
I know it's hard to know.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.450
That's a modeling figure.

00:18:04.450 --> 00:18:10.120
But I do think that in our current state if
we follow our current track that the problems

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:14.090
of antibiotic resistance will just become
more common they'll become more frequent.

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:17.220
SIERRA: Why do you think these numbers aren’t
more well-known?

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:24.769
TALKINGTON: I think the issue of antibiotic
resistance is a complex one for your average

00:18:24.769 --> 00:18:27.259
person to fully comprehend.

00:18:27.259 --> 00:18:32.720
It’s a lot easier to understand cancer,
and you see the number of people that are

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.480
directly affected by that.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:39.340
You have an advocacy group of people with
these specific diseases.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:44.429
The challenge with antibiotic resistance is
it’s underlying so many of these.

00:18:44.429 --> 00:18:50.620
It doesn’t always rise to the top, to people’s
awareness of the reason for illness or death.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:57.279
SIERRA: It sounds like it needs a good celebrity
spokesperson to get out there and explain

00:18:57.279 --> 00:18:58.279
everything.

00:18:58.279 --> 00:18:59.769
TALKINGTON: Possibly, yeah.

00:18:59.769 --> 00:19:04.500
SIERRA: What are some of the most important
changes that you would like to see?

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:11.749
TALKINGTON: We want to address this issue
from two basic areas: one is on stewardship.

00:19:11.749 --> 00:19:20.120
We have to figure out how to provide incentives,
both in animal agriculture and in human medicine

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:25.539
in the United States and around the globe
to ensure that we’re using these antibiotics

00:19:25.539 --> 00:19:27.200
only when they are necessary.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:33.879
We need to preserve these life-saving drugs
so that when we do need them, they work.

00:19:33.879 --> 00:19:39.740
At the same time, we have to put focus on
the innovation of new therapies.

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:47.850
We have to figure out a way to provide incentives
for industry or create some mechanism that

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:53.059
we ensure we have antibiotics to treat the
issues we have now and those that we will

00:19:53.059 --> 00:19:55.539
have in the years to come.

00:19:55.539 --> 00:20:03.600
SIERRA: So it sounds like a lot of influence
and power rests in the hands of government,

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:08.409
of drug developers and scientists, and of
farmers.

00:20:08.409 --> 00:20:09.409
What can we do?

00:20:09.409 --> 00:20:15.100
TALKINGTON: What we can do as individuals
is, when we go to the doctor, have that important

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:19.919
conversation about do I have a bacterial infection?

00:20:19.919 --> 00:20:22.389
Is an antibiotic the best treatment?

00:20:22.389 --> 00:20:27.649
Don’t demand an antibiotic if you have a
viral infection like a cold.

00:20:27.649 --> 00:20:33.400
There are things we can do in terms of making
sure when we are eating meat that we cook

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:40.030
it properly and wash our hands, but also becoming
aware of which companies, for example, are

00:20:40.030 --> 00:20:46.429
trying to make sure that the proteins that
they are buying are raised responsibly.

00:20:46.429 --> 00:20:51.990
We have to get the attention of those that
can make these important decisions about the

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.379
allocation of resources.

00:20:53.379 --> 00:20:55.899
We need money for research and development.

00:20:55.899 --> 00:21:00.960
We need to figure out how to get these innovations
to the patient bedside.

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:05.360
SIERRA: So it sounds like we just have to
be better educated, ask better questions,

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.970
and take Vitamin C instead of an antibiotic.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:08.970
TALKINGTON: Wash your hands.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:11.669
SIERRA: And wash our hands!

00:21:11.669 --> 00:21:19.850
Flemming: It is only five years since penicillin
was available to the public.

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:26.549
And in that short time, penicillin and other
antibiotics have made it necessary to rewrite

00:21:26.549 --> 00:21:29.690
all the textbooks of medical treatment.

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:31.490
Things have moved very rapidly.

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:37.249
They are still moving very rapidly and before
another five years have passed.

00:21:37.249 --> 00:21:43.120
There will be other new antibiotics, which
will extend the scope and bring other infections

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:44.460
under control.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:52.720
When penicillin first came to light neither
I nor anyone else had the slightest conception

00:21:52.720 --> 00:22:01.370
of the enormous influence that it and the
later antibiotics would have on medical practice.

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:22.330
SIERRA: There is a lot more to learn about
antibiotic resistance, more than we could

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:23.869
cover in our short window.

